        "id": 14570
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-05-15T20:41:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "These sections on Handler methods all begin by putting the Handler method in context by pointing out its relationship to the user-facing Logger methods. They hark back to statements in the earlier \"Loggers and their handlers\" section. I\u0027d like to maintain the parallelism here. What bothers you about this sentence? Can we reword it but keep it near the beginning of the section?\n\nFWIW, I don\u0027t see see Logger.With as a convenience wrapper around Handler.WithAttrs. The first method is part of the frontend, the second part of the backend. If this were a relationship of convenience, then one would think a user might have reason to call Handler.WithAttrs; but they never should.\n\nI see why you might think that though: Logger.With takes ...any and Handler.WithAttrs takes []Attr. What is missing is Logger.WithAttrs, a frontend method that takes ...Attr. That is a deliberate omission: Logger.With is not on the critical path, so it doesn\u0027t need a low-alloc variant like Logger.LogAttrs. You can still pass individual Attrs to Logger.With if you want. Although admittedly you can\u0027t use a []Attr. If people wanted to to pass []Attrs to Logger.With I would add Logger.WithAttrs, not tell them  to use Handler.WithAttrs.",
      "parentUuid": "e8ba7f35_cfde080a",
